{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good-cause eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only on the statutory grounds listed in the Anti-Eviction Act, after serving the Notice to Quit that each ground requires; no-fault removals require relocation assistance first.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings and complexes, and rooming and boarding homes. Hotel or motel guests covered only if they have no alternate residence and stay continually.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Also covers rooming and boarding homes; hotel/motel guests covered if no alternate residence and continual stay"},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; transient hotel, motel or guest-house guests; and a unit held in trust for a permanently occupying immediate family member who is developmentally disabled.","penalty":"If required relocation assistance is not paid and the municipality has no relocation fund, interest accrues at 18% per year and the amount due is a lien on the property.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes. This law also applies to rooming and boarding homes (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-53 to -84).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord or agent may not evict by self-help: no entering without consent or a court judgment, padlocking, changing locks, cutting utilities or blocking entry; only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental dwellings in New Jersey; the prohibition applies to landlords and anyone acting on their behalf.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord or other person who enters without a court order or unlawfully holds a tenant's belongings may be liable for damages; a tenant illegally evicted may recover possession, damages, court costs and reasonable attorney fees; the landlord may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"A landlord or their agent may not padlock, disconnect utilities or otherwise block entry to a rental premise while the tenant still lives there.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Unconscionability limit on residential rent increases","requirement":"A rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with all other laws and municipal ordinances, including local rent control; a tenant may withhold the amount of the increase while a court decides.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; increases must also comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they exist.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"The landlord may sue for eviction if the tenant fails to pay the increase; the court determines whether the increase is unconscionable.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with all other laws or municipal ordinances, including rent control.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
